Credit should be given to those who try their best to discover what they are supposed to know.
Quoting @j_foerst
@HeinrichKuttler I don't know about these claims, but blocking off travel for a country because they *discovered* a mutation is likely sending the wrong signal / incentives. It'd be a lot more logical to block travel from countries that don't do a required minimum level of sequencing..

Wow, glad that you like it! @martijnende. I always think that understanding how representation is learned (and how deep models achieve that) is super important.
Quoting @martijnende
@SuryaGanguli @tydsh @endernewton @facebookai I just finished reading your preprint, and I think it was brilliant! The field could definitely use more theoretical understanding of empirical observations, and this study does that in a very elegant way

What I learn from "Richard Sutton's bitter lesson" is not just "more compute works", but rethink how human knowledge should be put to AI. Instead of specifying latent vars and asking AI to estimate the parameters, to find/design mechanism to auto-discover the latents is the key.
Quoting @tydsh
@artistexyz @ylecun @MirowskiPiotr @DeepMind @OpenAI It is important to realize that humans have limitations and cannot explicitly enumerate all hidden variables / relationship in data. Instead, they should be automatically learned (e.g., by deep networks), and our goal is to devise a learning mechanism to make this happen.

If you want to learn precise physics, I bet there needs to be active learning (or on-policy RL, or whatever we name it) to explore fine structures of physics (e.g., interaction between objects, hard contact), while for movie, intriguing plots are way more important than precise physics.
Quoting @yuxili99
@tydsh Is video enough to learn a world model? Is embodiment required?

